# GSP1087 – Accelerating Analytical Queries using the AlloyDB Columnar Engine
Paste each block into Cloud Shell, in the same tab. Use the copy button.
## 1. Setup

Wait until lab-instance shows Ready on the AlloyDB page, then:

```bash
export ALLOYDB=$(gcloud alloydb instances list --filter="name~lab-instance" --format="value(ipAddress)" | head -1)
export VM_ZONE=$(gcloud compute instances list --filter="name=alloydb-client" --format="value(zone.basename())")
echo $ALLOYDB $VM_ZONE
```

## 2. Task 1: baseline dataset (3–5 min)

```bash
gcloud compute ssh alloydb-client --zone=$VM_ZONE --quiet --command="echo $ALLOYDB > alloydbip.txt; export PGPASSWORD=Change3Me; pgbench -h $ALLOYDB -U postgres -i -s 500 -F 90 -n postgres; psql -h $ALLOYDB -U postgres -c 'select count(*) from pgbench_accounts;'"
```

Expect a count of 50000000.

Check my progress: Create a baseline data set

## 3. Task 2: baseline test

```bash
cat > baseline.sql <<'SQL'
\timing on
SELECT aid, bid, abalance FROM pgbench_accounts WHERE bid < 189 OR abalance > 100 LIMIT 20;
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE,COSTS,SETTINGS,BUFFERS,TIMING,SUMMARY,WAL,VERBOSE) SELECT count(*) FROM pgbench_accounts WHERE bid < 189 OR abalance > 100;
SQL
gcloud compute ssh alloydb-client --zone=$VM_ZONE --quiet --command="PGPASSWORD=Change3Me psql -h $ALLOYDB -U postgres -P pager=off" < baseline.sql | grep -E "Execution Time|Planning Time|ERROR"
```

Note the Execution Time (around 10,000 ms).

## 4. Tasks 3–5: enable and test the Columnar Engine

```bash
cat > columnar.sql <<'SQL'
SHOW google_columnar_engine.enabled;
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS google_columnar_engine;
SELECT google_columnar_engine_add('pgbench_accounts');
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE,COSTS,SETTINGS,BUFFERS,TIMING,SUMMARY,WAL,VERBOSE) SELECT count(*) FROM pgbench_accounts WHERE bid < 189 OR abalance > 100;
SQL
gcloud compute ssh alloydb-client --zone=$VM_ZONE --quiet --command="PGPASSWORD=Change3Me psql -h $ALLOYDB -U postgres -P pager=off" < columnar.sql | grep -E "^ on|google_columnar|Columnar|Execution Time|ERROR"
```

Expect `on`, a Columnar Scan, and an Execution Time under 100 ms.

